The constant $k = 4 > 0$, so the branches lie in Quadrants $1$ and $3$.
The vertical asymptote is $x = 0$ and the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-6, xmax=6, ymin=-6, ymax=6, samples=200, clip=false, restrict y to domain=-6.5:6.5, unbounded coords=jump, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2,4}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4}]
\addplot[dashed, red, thick] coordinates {(0,-5.5) (0,5.5)};
\addplot[dashed, red, thick] coordinates {(-5.5,0) (5.5,0)};
\node[left, red] at (axis cs:-0.2,5.0) {$x=0$};
\node[above, red] at (axis cs:3.0,0.15) {$y=0$};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=0.8:5.5] {4/x};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-5.5:-0.8] {4/x};
\node[blue] at (axis cs:3.5,3.5) {Quadrant $1$};
\node[blue] at (axis cs:-3.5,-3.5) {Quadrant $3$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

At $x = 2$:
For $y = \dfrac{2}{x}$, $y = 1$.
For $y = \dfrac{6}{x}$, $y = 3$.
Since $3 > 1$, the curve $y = \dfrac{6}{x}$ is positioned further from both axes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.8, xmax=7.5, ymin=-0.8, ymax=7.5, samples=200, clip=false, restrict y to domain=-1:8, unbounded coords=jump, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={2,4,6}, ytick={2,4,6}]
\addplot[dashed, gray, thick] coordinates {(0,0) (0,7)};
\addplot[dashed, gray, thick] coordinates {(0,0) (7,0)};
\node[above left, gray] at (axis cs:0,6.6) {$x=0$};
\node[below right, gray] at (axis cs:6.6,0) {$y=0$};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=0.4:6.8] {2/x};
\addplot[thick, red, <->, domain=1:6.8] {6/x};
\node[below right, blue] at (axis cs:4.2,0.48) {$y=\dfrac{2}{x}$};
\node[above right, red] at (axis cs:4.4,1.36) {$y=\dfrac{6}{x}$};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(2,1)};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, red] coordinates {(2,3)};
\draw[dotted, gray] (axis cs:2,0) -- (axis cs:2,3);
\node[right, blue] at (axis cs:2,1) {$(2,1)$};
\node[right, red] at (axis cs:2,3) {$(2,3)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
